# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js project following the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart).

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` package (version 1.1.0) using npm
- Updated `package.json` with the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Code Configuration
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next';`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component to the root layout body, positioned after the existing `<Analytics />` component

## Implementation Details

### Framework: Next.js (App Router)
The project uses Next.js with the App Router pattern (version 16.3.8). Following the official documentation, I added the SpeedInsights component to the root layout file (`app/layout.tsx`) which is the recommended approach for Next.js 13.5+.

### Integration Pattern
The SpeedInsights component is placed at the same level as the existing Vercel Analytics component, ensuring both monitoring services work together seamlessly. The component is imported from `@vercel/speed-insights/next` which is the Next.js-specific export that provides optimal integration with the framework.

## Verification

✅ **Build Test**: Successfully ran `npm run build` - the project compiles without errors  
✅ **TypeScript**: Type checking passed during build  
✅ **Dependencies**: Lock file updated correctly  
⚠️ **Linter**: Pre-existing ESLint configuration issue (unrelated to changes)  
ℹ️ **Tests**: No test suite configured in the project  

## Files Modified
1. `app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
2. `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
3. `package-lock.json` - Updated lock file with new dependencies

## Next Steps
Once deployed to Vercel, Speed Insights will automatically begin collecting Core Web Vitals data from real user visits. The metrics can be viewed in the Vercel dashboard under the Speed Insights section of the project.

## Notes
- The component placement in the root layout ensures Speed Insights tracks all pages in the application
- The integration is minimal and non-invasive, requiring no additional configuration
- Speed Insights works alongside the existing Vercel Analytics integration
- The tracking script will only load in production deployments on Vercel
